input.content:
---
name: LCD rotation envelope around screen paints
description: Any M5.Lcd paint outside GFXScreen::loop() must wrap itself in setRotation(3)/setRotation(1) or it renders 180° flipped
type: project
---

GFXScreen::loop() in `bgeigiezen_firmware/gfx_screen.cpp` brackets every render tick with `M5.Lcd.startWrite(); M5.Lcd.setRotation(3); ...draw...; M5.Lcd.setRotation(1); M5.Lcd.display(); M5.Lcd.endWrite();`. Between ticks the panel sits at rotation 1, which is 180° flipped from the orientation the UI is designed for (rotation 3 = the "buttons at the bottom" view the user actually holds).

Any paint that happens *outside* that envelope — e.g. progress feedback emitted from `handle_input` before a synchronous blocking call (HTTP upload, long file write, etc.) — sees rotation 1 and renders upside-down and mirror-positioned relative to the rest of the screen.

**Why:** Diagnosed during SD log upload work — the manual "Uploading..." paint in `LogViewerScreen::upload_detail` was misplaced and inverted because the upload runs synchronously from `handle_input`, never re-entering the render loop. Took several rounds to find since the symptom (mirrored text) doesn't point to rotation directly.

**How to apply:** When adding any out-of-band M5.Lcd paint (mid-blocking-operation feedback, splash messages from non-render callbacks, etc.), wrap it with the same envelope:

```cpp
M5.Lcd.startWrite();
M5.Lcd.setRotation(3);
// ...paint...
M5.Lcd.setRotation(1);
M5.Lcd.display();
M5.Lcd.endWrite();
```

If the paint is something the regular `render_*` path already handles (e.g. a status field tied to a state enum), prefer refactoring so the blocking call yields back to the render loop instead — it's cheaper and keeps drawing in one place.

